{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-13_10-13-13-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nupd={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Small closing contribution with the remaining allocation: no new execution and no new suspicion, just a durable record of an observation the two witnesses already support and that a reviewer needs when judging the findings - in two of them the API's own documentation promises a protection the code does not provide, and the caller-visible result is a successful future. Each entry cites one receipt so no joint-history reasoning is needed.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n  {\n   \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@addvoter-doc-promises-staging-but-installs-voter\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"AddVoter's documentation still describes the deprecated staged behaviour ('will add the given server to the cluster as a staging server ... The leader will promote the staging server to a voter once that server is ready'), and BootstrapCluster's documentation recommends growing a cluster exactly that way (bootstrap one voter, then AddVoter the next server). The code does neither: appendConfigurationEntry installs the named server as a voter in configurations.latest and in the commitment map immediately, so in the documented 1-to-2 growth step the surviving node's lease quorum and election quorum already require the new server. The caller-visible result is a successful AddVoter future, so the API reports success for a step that has just made the cluster unavailable until that server runs.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Protocol version 3, single-voter configuration grown by one voter, named server not running; witnessed with test timeouts by receipt e19ad6d076fa4d878b7bd0f90f067fca (indices 8-11), where the configuration entry committed, the future returned nil, the node became a Candidate and eventually recovered only after the named server was started.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"addvoter-doc-staging-promise\\\",\\\"bootstrapcluster-growth-guidance\\\",\\\"appendconfiguration-installs-latest\\\",\\\"checkleaderlease-latest-quorum\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"e19ad6d076fa4d878b7bd0f90f067fca\\\":[8,9,10,11]}\n  },\n  {\n   \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@transfer-doc-allows-ineligible-target\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"LeadershipTransferToServer's documentation offers to transfer leadership to 'a specific server in the cluster' and promises that a transfer which cannot complete 'will fail gracefully', while the code path accepts any server for which replication state exists (including a Nonvoter) and the protocol refuses to elect a non-voter candidate. Naming a non-voter therefore deposes the incumbent leader without transferring anything, and the future can still report success, so neither the documented eligibility restriction nor the documented graceful failure is what the caller receives.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Protocol version 3, three voters plus a caught-up Nonvoter added through AddNonvoter; witnessed by receipt 79cf4ec11d694dfbaeb78ac8b07e1f67 (indices 8-9), where the transfer future returned nil while the target never became Leader and the incumbent stepped down.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"api-transfer-to-server\\\",\\\"initiate-transfer-check\\\",\\\"leaderloop-transfer-target\\\",\\\"electself-voters-only\\\",\\\"requestvote-highterm-stepdown-nonvoter-reject\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"79cf4ec11d694dfbaeb78ac8b07e1f67\\\":[8,9]}\n  }\n ]\n}\nopen('update.json','w').write(json.dumps(upd,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nprint('ok', len(json.load(open('update.json'))['knowledge']))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
